#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# dev/regression-set — the first part of the regression set: THE PREVIOUS RELEASE'S OWN
# TEST SUITE, RUN AGAINST THE CANDIDATE'S BINARY (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-06, "The
# regression set's first part, as a tool"; implementation/stabilization-workflow.md ->
# The regression set).
#
# The release condition's second clause is "a working product others can rely on", and
# its instrument is a regression set: no command that works on the previous release stops
# working. The suite a release shipped with is what that release was held to. So this
# tool builds that suite at the release's commit, runs it once on its own binary, puts
# the candidate's binary where those tests look for theirs, and runs the same test
# binaries again. A test that passed on the release's binary and fails on the candidate's
# is a difference; a difference is intended only when a row of a committed list says so
# and points at the commit or the ruling that made it. Any other difference is red. And a
# test that fails on its own binary drops out of the comparison only by a row of the same
# list, which says why and points at the record of a run that showed it.
#
# IT IS NOT A TEST FRAMEWORK. It defines no test and runs none itself: the tests are the
# previous release's, built by cargo and run by cargo-nextest, the runner the gate uses
# (implementation/pinning.md -> "No script-runner harness"). What is here is the swap,
# the proof of which binary ran, and the comparison of two of that runner's reports.
#
# THE TRAP, AND WHY THE RUNS ARE MADE AS THEY ARE. The tests name their binary at compile
# time (`env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_jigc")`): one path, inside the previous release's target
# directory. After the candidate's binary is put there, a plain `cargo nextest run`
# puts the old one back before it runs a test — cargo finds the unit fresh and copies
# `deps/jigc-<hash>` over the path again, printing nothing — and the run reads green: the
# baseline, twice (measured 2026-10-06). So NO RUN HERE GOES THROUGH CARGO'S BUILD. The
# test binaries are built once; the build is then recorded (`cargo nextest list
# --list-type binaries-only`, `cargo metadata`) and BOTH runs are made from that record
# (`--binaries-metadata`, `--cargo-metadata`), the reuse-build path, which starts no
# compiler and copies no file.
#
# WHICH BINARY RAN IS PROVEN, NOT ASSUMED. The path is read out of the recorded build,
# and at least one test binary must hold it in its bytes. The file at that path is hashed
# before and after each run: the previous release's binary around the baseline, the
# candidate's around the second run. A hash that is not the expected one — before or
# after — makes the whole run VOID, never green; so does a candidate whose binary is byte
# for byte the previous release's, since no hash could then tell the two runs apart.
#
# Usage — every call prints ONE line of JSON on stdout, whatever it exits with:
#
#   dev/regression-set run --previous SHA --candidate SHA --list PATH --scratch DIR
#                      [--keep] [--baseline DIR]
#       The whole check. SHA is a full commit sha, and the two are two commits; the
#       candidate and the previous release are built from `git archive` of their commits,
#       never from the working tree. PATH is the list's path in the repository: it is read
#       out of the candidate's commit, never from the working tree either. DIR exists and
#       lies outside the repository; everything is done in one directory minted there. In
#       order:
#         1. the list is read (below) and recorded, by its path and its hash — a list that
#            is refused costs no build;
#         2. the previous release is unpacked and its tests built, `--locked`, in the dev
#            profile, into a target directory of its own; the build is recorded;
#         3. the candidate is unpacked and its binary built, `--locked`, the same profile,
#            into another target directory;
#         4. the baseline: the recorded test binaries, on the previous release's binary;
#         5. the swap — a new file renamed over the path, never a write into the old one —
#            and the same test binaries again;
#         6. the verdict, as `verdict` gives it over what the run left.
#       A step that cannot be done ends the run there, void. Unless --keep is given, the
#       two source trees and the two target directories are removed at the end; the
#       evidence stays. With --baseline DIR what steps 2 and 4 make is KEPT in DIR — where
#       the run that made it compared against it whole — and a later run that names DIR
#       uses it in their place (A KEPT BASELINE, below). Without it nothing is kept,
#       nothing is asked for a key, and the run is the one above.
#
#   dev/regression-set verdict --evidence DIR --list PATH
#       The verdict over the evidence a run left, and no build and no test: the same
#       function `run` ends with. DIR holds `run.json` — the two commits, the list the run
#       read, the two binaries' hashes, the path, the four hashes taken at it, and a step
#       that failed — and the runner's two reports, `baseline-junit.xml` and
#       `candidate-junit.xml`. PATH is read out of the candidate's commit the evidence
#       names, and must be the list the run read: a verdict over another list is void.
#
#   dev/regression-set check-list --previous SHA --candidate SHA --list PATH
#       The list alone, read as `run` reads it. Prints {status: listed, list}.
#
# THE LIST OF INTENDED CHANGES. A file OF THE CANDIDATE'S COMMIT, named by its path in the
# repository — plain segments, no `..`, not absolute — and read out of that commit: what
# the working tree has at the path, and any file no commit holds, is never read. So a
# green rests on a list the candidate itself carries, and the line says which: its path,
# the commit, and the sha256 of its bytes. It is text, UTF-8, one row per line; a line that
# is empty or begins `#` is no row. A row is FOUR fields separated by ONE TAB each:
#
#     <binary>  <test>  <what changed, in one line>  <pointer>
#
#   binary   the test binary, as the runner names it (`jigc::g_flow`, `jigc-engine`)
#   test     the test's path inside it (`setup::setup_writes_the_marker`)
#   pointer  what made the change intended — one of
#              commit:<full sha>        a commit the candidate holds and the previous
#                                       release does not
#              ruling:<path>#<heading>  a file of the candidate's commit, by its path in
#                                       the repository, and the opening words of exactly
#                                       one of its markdown headings
#
# AN EXCLUSION ROW is a row of the same list and of the same four fields, for a test that
# FAILS ON ITS OWN BINARY — the previous release's test, on the previous release's binary.
# Its third field says why it fails there, and its pointer is of a kind of its own:
#
#              excluded:<path>          a file of the candidate's commit that is the line
#                                       of a run of this tool — against the same previous
#                                       release — in which the test failed on its own
#                                       binary: named there under `excluded` or
#                                       `excluded_by_no_row`
#
# No field is empty or has space around it, and no test is listed twice. A ROW THAT
# POINTS AT NOTHING IS REFUSED, and with it the whole list: a sha that is no commit, a
# commit outside `previous..candidate`, a file the candidate does not have, a heading no
# line or more than one line opens with, a record that is no line of this tool, is of a
# run against another release, or does not name the test. The tool reads the list; no
# agent does.
#
# THE VERDICT. Per test, by binary and name, from the two reports. Both runs are made
# with one retry: a test counts as failed only when it failed twice in one run, and one
# that passed on its retry is named under `flaky`.
#
#   green   every test that passed on the previous release's binary and fails on the
#           candidate's is on the list — none at all included — and every test that
#           failed on its own binary is on an exclusion row
#   red     at least one difference is not on the list: each is named under `not_on_list`
#   void    the check did not establish either. `reason` is one word, `detail` a sentence:
#             did-not-run      a tool it needs is not on PATH
#             build-previous   the previous release could not be unpacked, its tests did
#                              not build, or the recorded build names no `jigc` binary
#             build-candidate  the candidate could not be unpacked or did not build
#             baseline         the baseline cannot be compared against: the file at the
#                              path was not the previous release's binary around it, it
#                              left no readable report or it ran no test — or, with every
#                              difference on the list, a test failed on its own binary
#                              that no exclusion row names: each is named under
#                              `excluded_by_no_row`
#             swap             the swap did not hold: the two binaries are the same
#                              bytes, no test binary names the path, or the file at the
#                              path was not the candidate's binary before or after the run
#                              — the previous release's put back among them
#             incomplete       the second run left no readable report, or did not run
#                              exactly the tests the baseline ran
#             evidence         `run.json` is missing, unreadable or lacks a fact, or the
#                              list it says the run read is not, by its hash, the one the
#                              verdict is asked over
#
# A TEST THAT FAILS ON ITS OWN BINARY is no evidence about the candidate, and it is
# coverage the check does not have. It drops out of the comparison ONLY BY AN EXCLUSION
# ROW: with its row it is named under `excluded`, with the row; with none it is named
# under `excluded_by_no_row`, and the run is not green — red where a difference is off the
# list too, since that much is established whatever the baseline lacks, and void
# otherwise. No number of such tests is tolerated unnamed, and none is too many once each
# has its row. So in a green line `excluded` is exactly the exclusion rows that matched.
#
# A ROW THAT MATCHES NOTHING is stale and is named under `stale` with why. A row for a
# change: `no-such-test`, `failed-on-its-own-binary`, `passed-on-the-candidate`. An
# exclusion row: `no-such-test`, `passed-on-its-own-binary`. Stale rows do not change the
# verdict; they are reported so that a list cannot quietly outlive what it excuses.
#
# A KEPT BASELINE. About half of a run depends on the previous release's commit alone:
# its tree, its built tests, and their run on their own binary. `run --baseline DIR`
# keeps those in DIR — a directory outside the repository, made if it is not there — and
# a later run that names DIR builds the candidate's binary, swaps it in at the path inside
# the kept baseline, and makes the second run alone. The swap and its proof are the same:
# the two hashes around the baseline's run are the ones taken when it was made, the two
# around the candidate's run are this run's, and the evidence of the run is as whole as a
# fresh run's — the baseline's report and the record of the build are copied into it.
#
# IT IS OF THE CLASS THAT RECORDS FALSELY. A baseline that is not the one this run would
# have made, used as if it were, is a comparison against nothing. So a kept baseline is
# never used because a directory is there, or is named for it: it is kept WITH A KEY, in a
# record that is written last, and used only while every member of the key is what it was
# kept with. They are held in this order, and the first that differs refuses it:
#
#   previous    the previous release's commit
#   exclusions  the list's exclusion rows, in no order. Each says that a test fails on
#               its own binary, which is a claim about the baseline's run: other claims
#               are held to a run of their own, never to a report made before them
#   tool        this file's bytes: what a baseline is and how it is held is written here
#   runner      what `cargo nextest --version` says: the runner made the report
#   compiler    what `rustc -vV` says IN THE PREVIOUS RELEASE'S TREE — the compiler as that
#               tree resolves it today, which is not what its toolchain file asks for
#   git         what `git --version` says, of the git the tests are run with: they drive
#               it, and which of them fail on their own binary depends on it
#   tests       the sha256 of every test binary the recorded build names: the second run
#               is made with these files, and is compared with what the same files did
#   jigc        the sha256 of the baseline's own binary — of the file at the path the
#               tests name, and of the kept copy that path is put back from
#
# And two words stand before the key, for a directory that is no finished baseline at
# all: `incomplete` — it holds no finished record of this tool's: a run ended, or was
# killed, before its baseline held; `record` — the kept tree does not hold a file of the
# commit the baseline was kept for as git holds it, a kept file (the baseline's report,
# the two records of the build) is not the bytes it was kept with, or the recorded build
# names a path outside this directory: the tests have their binary's path compiled in, so
# a kept baseline is used where it was built.
#
# REFUSED IS NOT VOID. A baseline that is refused is removed, the previous release is
# built afresh in its place, in the same run, and that baseline is kept where it is fit
# (below); the run gives its verdict as any run does. So DIR holds one baseline, the last
# one a run kept.
#
# FIT TO KEEP: A BASELINE IS KEPT ONLY BY THE RUN THAT COMPARED AGAINST IT WHOLE. What a
# run builds in DIR is held until that run has its line, and the record is written after
# the verdict — where the run ended GREEN OR RED and names NO TEST under
# `excluded_by_no_row`. So the baseline that is kept ran on its own binary, left a
# readable report, its second run ran exactly its tests, and every test that failed in it
# has its row. A run that ended void — whatever by — or that names such a test keeps
# nothing: DIR is left without a record, the next run finds it unfinished (`incomplete`),
# removes it and builds afresh. The other choice was made until 2026-10-13, and kept a
# baseline as soon as its two hashes held and its report was readable: a test that failed
# once on its own binary, on a loaded machine, or a runner that was signalled while it
# ran — it writes a report of the tests that had run, and exits as for a failed test —
# was then kept with the baseline, held by its key like any other, and voided EVERY later
# run that named DIR (`baseline`, `incomplete`) until the directory was removed by hand.
# AND SO A VOID ON A BASELINE THAT WAS USED IS NOT THE KEPT DIRECTORY'S DOING, but for
# what a kept baseline is not held to (below): the run that kept it compared against the
# same report, with the same test binaries, whole.
#
# AT REST a kept baseline is as its own baseline left it, with the previous release's
# binary at the path the tests name: a run puts it back there when it is over, after the
# last hash of its proof is taken. A run that is killed after its swap leaves the
# candidate's binary there, and the next run refuses the baseline — by `jigc` where it
# was kept before that run, and by `incomplete` where that run built it: its record was
# not written yet.
#
# TWO RUNS DO NOT SHARE ONE KEPT BASELINE AT A TIME — the second would drive the first
# one's candidate. DIR has a lock, held by a run from its start to its end and let go by
# the system when its process is gone. A run that does not get it says `locked`, and is
# the run it would be without --baseline: a build of its own, in its own directory, and
# nothing read or written in DIR.
#
# WHAT THE LINE SAYS OF IT — `baseline: {dir, used, refused, detail, sha256, unkept}`, in
# the line of a run that names DIR and in no other: `used`, whether the baseline kept there
# was used and the previous release not built; `refused`, the word by which a baseline
# that was there was not used — a member of the key, `incomplete`, `record` or `locked` —
# with its sentence in `detail`, which says what the member is now and what the baseline
# was kept with; `sha256`, the sha256 of the key of the baseline the run left in DIR, null
# where it left none — NAMED AS EVERY OTHER HASH OF THE LINE IS, and never `key`: a record
# commits this line, and gitleaks' `generic-api-key` reads a `key` beside a 64-hex value as
# a credential, so under that name no record of a run with a regression item could be
# written (the staged canary's part 2, 2026-10-15), while the record kept in DIR, which no
# record commits, holds the key itself under `key`, an object no rule reads as a secret;
# and `unkept`, null where the run used the kept baseline, left one
# there or was shut out (`locked`) — else {why, detail}: WHAT THE RUN BUILT THERE WAS NOT
# KEPT, by the word the run was void by, or `baseline` for a test no row excludes, and
# in a sentence. So a void line says of itself whether the kept directory can be its
# cause: `used: true`, and it compared against a baseline another run kept; `used: false`,
# and it built its own, which the next run does not meet. The evidence holds the record
# itself, `baseline.json`, where one was used or kept, and the line's `seconds` what
# holding the key and keeping took, as `kept`.
#
# WHAT A KEPT BASELINE IS NOT HELD TO. The environment beside these: a variable the build
# or a test reads, the system under them. The runner and git by more than what they say of
# themselves. A file in the kept tree that the commit does not hold — what a test left
# there — and the target directory beyond the test binaries and the one `jigc`. And the
# record itself, which is what everything else is held to.
#
# The line: {tool, status, reason, detail, previous: {commit, sha256}, candidate:
# {commit, sha256}, list: {path, commit, sha256, rows}, counts: {tests, baseline_passed,
# baseline_failed, candidate_passed, candidate_failed, differences, on_list, not_on_list,
# excluded, excluded_by_no_row, stale, fixed}, not_on_list: [{binary, test}], on_list:
# [{binary, test, change, pointer}], excluded: [{binary, test, change, pointer}],
# excluded_by_no_row: [{binary, test}], stale: [{binary, test, pointer, why}], flaky:
# {baseline: [...], candidate: [...]}, proof: {baked_path, named_by, baseline_before,
# baseline_after, candidate_before, candidate_after}, seconds, evidence, work} — and
# `baseline`, as above, where the run named a directory for one. A void line carries what
# was established before it — and `excluded_by_no_row`, where that is what voided it; a
# refusal prints {tool, status: refused, refused: WORD, detail}.
#
# WHAT A GREEN RESULT ESTABLISHES, AND WHAT IT CANNOT SEE. Green says: nothing the
# previous release's suite pinned THROUGH THE BINARY moved, but for the listed rows. It
# is a floor, never "nothing that worked stopped working":
#   - a test that runs in its own process — a unit test, a test that calls the library —
#     never reaches the binary, and the swap cannot reach it (about half the suite);
#   - it sees only the states the previous release's tests built: a regression in a
#     layout or a configuration no old test sets up is invisible (the second part of the
#     regression set, the success paths across configurations and layouts, is for those —
#     and is not built);
#   - a candidate whose package version was bumped fails the old tests that compare the
#     binary's version with their own: run it before the bump, or list them;
#   - where an old test hands the binary the previous release's pack on disk, the
#     candidate runs with that pack and not its own;
#   - one platform — the one it runs on — and one profile, dev.
#
# Exit status, and the word a refusal opens with — `regression-set: refused <word>: …`,
# one line on stderr:
#
#    0  green
#    1  red
#    2  usage            an unknown act or flag, or a missing argument
#    3  list             the list is not a file of the candidate's commit or not in its
#                        form, or a row points at nothing
#    4  commit           a commit named is not the full sha of a commit this repository
#                        holds, or the two commits are one
#    5  scratch          the scratch directory does not exist, or it or the directory
#                        of a kept baseline lies inside the repository
#   10  did-not-run      void, as above — and so each of the next six
#   11  build-previous
#   12  build-candidate
#   13  baseline
#   14  swap
#   15  incomplete
#   16  evidence
#
# Needs python3 and git; `run` needs cargo, cargo-nextest and tar beside them, and rustc
# where it names a kept baseline. It finds its repository from where it lies, never from
# where it was called.
# tooling-tests/dev_regression_set.rs drives it.

